1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,790
Sutra runs a snack factory, from flour sack to GST invoice.

2
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:08,868
It is an ERP for a South Indian packaged-snack maker.

3
00:00:08,918 --> 00:00:14,717
Salesmen take orders on their phones, the factory makes murukku to a recipe,

4
00:00:14,767 --> 00:00:20,796
every stock movement lands in one ledger, and the office invoices and collects.

5
00:00:20,846 --> 00:00:26,490
The landing page explains all 13 factory departments in a one-minute tour.

6
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:34,481
Five roles share one database, each with its own workspace: Owner, Admin, Salesman,

7
00:00:34,531 --> 00:00:37,464
Production Supervisor and Accountant.

8
00:00:37,514 --> 00:00:42,543
Every page and every API call checks permissions on the server.

9
00:00:42,593 --> 00:00:47,218
The Owner's overview shows sales, production yield, stock,

10
00:00:47,268 --> 00:00:52,700
cost per packet and receivables, all calculated from posted records.

11
00:00:54,050 --> 00:00:56,426
Salesman A works from a phone.

12
00:00:56,476 --> 00:01:01,683
Choose Retailer A, tap in 20 packets of Mixture, check the value,

13
00:01:01,733 --> 00:01:03,300
and place the order.

14
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:05,646
It takes well under a minute.

15
00:01:05,696 --> 00:01:10,983
If the network drops, the order waits on the phone and syncs once,

16
00:01:11,033 --> 00:01:12,520
without duplicates.

17
00:01:13,870 --> 00:01:19,971
In the office, the Admin sees the new order with a live stock check: requested,

18
00:01:20,021 --> 00:01:22,151
available, and any shortage.

19
00:01:22,201 --> 00:01:26,355
Quantities or rates can be corrected before invoicing,

20
00:01:26,405 --> 00:01:30,481
and the old and new values are kept in the audit log.

21
00:01:30,531 --> 00:01:34,530
Here the stock is sufficient, so the Admin approves.

22
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:46,912
For the benchmark, Retailer A ordered 150 packets of Murukku at ₹50.

23
00:01:46,962 --> 00:01:48,868
50 = ₹7,875.

24
00:01:48,918 --> 00:01:58,157
Invoices are numbered by financial year, download as PDF,

25
00:01:58,207 --> 00:02:05,980
and can only be cancelled through a credit note.

26
00:02:06,030 --> 00:02:14,780
Dispatch deducted the packets from stock exactly once.

27
00:02:16,130 --> 00:02:21,969
Purchases can start with a purchase order.

28
00:02:22,019 --> 00:02:23,511
125 per kg.

29
00:02:23,561 --> 00:02:31,362
Now we receive packaging against an open purchase order.

30
00:02:31,412 --> 00:02:36,971
The form is pre-filled with the balance,

31
00:02:37,021 --> 00:02:43,420
and posting adds the stock through the ledger.

32
00:02:44,770 --> 00:02:52,175
2 kg spice, 200 pouches and 10 cartons for 200 packets.

33
00:02:52,225 --> 00:03:03,560
5 L of oil and produced 188 good packets, with 6 pouches damaged and 9 cartons used.

34
00:03:03,610 --> 00:03:13,590
Completing the run posted every movement in a single database transaction.

35
00:03:14,940 --> 00:03:21,524
Planned versus actual consumption is calculated for every material.

36
00:03:21,574 --> 00:03:25,089
5 L against 10 L, a variance of +5%.

37
00:03:25,139 --> 00:03:30,040
That is beyond the 3% tolerance, so it is flagged.

38
00:03:31,390 --> 00:03:37,738
The yield was 188 packets against 200 expected: a variance of −6%.

39
00:03:37,788 --> 00:03:45,300
Damaged pouches are recorded separately as wastage, and they are still costed.

40
00:03:46,650 --> 00:03:56,318
Every stock change is a row in an append-only ledger, with the document, the user,

41
00:03:56,368 --> 00:04:00,228
and the balance before and after.

42
00:04:00,278 --> 00:04:03,073
5 L, and pouches at 306.

43
00:04:03,123 --> 00:04:10,420
Finished goods went from 50 to 238, then to 88 after dispatch.

44
00:04:11,770 --> 00:04:22,858
Costing uses the weighted average: flour ₹1,725, oil ₹1,470, spice ₹240,

45
00:04:22,908 --> 00:04:33,531
pouches ₹485 including the damaged ones, cartons ₹135, labour ₹1,200,

46
00:04:33,581 --> 00:04:36,471
gas and power ₹600.

47
00:04:36,521 --> 00:04:39,564
The run cost ₹5,855.

48
00:04:39,614 --> 00:04:41,730
14 per packet.

49
00:04:43,080 --> 00:04:46,207
Retailer A paid ₹4,000 by UPI.

50
00:04:46,257 --> 00:04:52,986
It is allocated against the invoice, leaving ₹3,875 outstanding,

51
00:04:53,036 --> 00:04:57,540
which also shows in the receivables ageing.

52
00:04:58,890 --> 00:05:02,850
Distributor B in Karnataka ordered 120 packets.

53
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:07,968
Only 88 are available, so the system shows a shortage of 32,

54
00:05:08,018 --> 00:05:13,257
and it will not invoice or dispatch stock that does not exist.

55
00:05:13,307 --> 00:05:19,570
Because Karnataka is another state, the invoice would charge IGST instead.

56
00:05:20,920 --> 00:05:26,994
The Accountant has read-only access to every register: sales, purchases,

57
00:05:27,044 --> 00:05:33,203
GST outward supplies, stock, production, costing, collections and ageing.

58
00:05:33,253 --> 00:05:38,477
Every report filters by date and exports to CSV, Excel or PDF,

59
00:05:38,527 --> 00:05:43,070
and the monthly audit report brings them all together.

60
00:05:44,420 --> 00:05:46,596
Finally, the 3D digital twin.

61
00:05:46,646 --> 00:05:51,509
Every department is coloured from the database: green is normal,

62
00:05:51,559 --> 00:05:57,880
amber needs attention, red is critical, and grey is idle, always with a text label.

63
00:05:57,930 --> 00:06:04,175
The finished-goods warehouse is red, because Distributor B is short by 32 packets.

64
00:06:04,225 --> 00:06:06,938
Frying is amber for the oil overrun.

65
00:06:06,988 --> 00:06:11,160
The packaging store shows the pouches we just received.

66
00:06:11,210 --> 00:06:15,382
Machines only move while a run is actually in progress,

67
00:06:15,432 --> 00:06:18,376
so right now the line is honestly idle.

68
00:06:18,426 --> 00:06:21,830
A guided tour walks through every department.

69
00:06:23,180 --> 00:06:30,009
The benchmark check reads the posted records back: 25 out of 25 checks pass,

70
00:06:30,059 --> 00:06:37,430
and 68 automated tests cover inventory, production, GST, payments and permissions.

71
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:42,680
That is Sutra: one ledger, from flour sack to GST invoice.
